Sculpt and rig the **Caldera River Tap**, the smallest, lowest waterworks of the Holdfast
— a low brass sluice gate spanning a river channel, a weir with a liftable gate in an iron
frame, with an undershot paddle wheel turning in the flow beside it, gravity-fed and simple
— as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by
**painting opaque cells** one operation at a time, and you **author its idle and draw
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the River Tap is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the weir into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every form are
**entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a sluice gate and paddle wheel need.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the ordered
operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with
the rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a
**part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels
are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** —
it appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render`
when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled
scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name gate
voxel-anim fill-box --part gate --x0 8 --y0 4 --z0 12 --x1 20 --y1 16 --z1 15 --color "#c8a24a"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining/keyframing animations), and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the
authoritative contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see
that part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
River Tap composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the gate seats in the frame
across the channel and the paddle wheel sits low in the flow beside it. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished Tap; the scene simply composes them there.

