## What to deliver

Build and rig the River Tap up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and the
rig is complete: a low brass sluice gate spanning a river channel, a weir with a liftable gate
in an iron frame, an undershot paddle wheel turning in the flow beside it, and a verdigris
accent on the sluice gate frame sculpted **only** in `#2f7d72` and appearing nowhere else on
the model. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation
subcommands: an **`idle`** (a gentle self-playing loop that turns the paddle wheel while the
gate stays still) and a **`draw`** (a steady loop that lifts the gate and spins the wheel up
as water is drawn, reading as active but simple and gravity-fed, distinct from the idle). The
animations and the accent region are the fixed contract — produce all of them; the parts and
joints that realize them are yours. You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on
top, but do not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `draw`. Before you finish, run
`voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an
unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part;
`voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of
an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs
and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
